Signal Plotter
--------------

We have overhauled the Ptolemy Plot package. The changes are backward
compatible on static plots, but not with live plots. It includes a new
application called PtolemyPlot that can open and save plot files. This
is the beginning of a more interactive signal viewer. The changes are:

 - Changed zoom out so it is not limited to the fill region.
   You can now zoom out arbitrarily far.

 - Fixed a bug where YTicks was not recognized in a file (XTicks was).

 - Fixed a bug where DataSet: with a blank name does not create a blank
   legend entry.

 - Use jdk 1.1 event model and font support everywhere, as well as jdk 1.1
   method names.

 - Used javadoc formatting throughout.

 - Fixed bug where auto-ranging depended on all data seen historically.
   Now it is recalculated if boundary points are erased.

 - Added a clear() method that clears the plot in preparation for new data.

 - Allow an arbitrary number of data sets, rather than limiting it to 64.

 - Cleaned up some questionable uses of threading.

 - Redesigned the interface to live, animated plots.


Filter Design
-------------

William Wu has modified his filter design applet (see
http://ptolemy.eecs.berkeley.edu/~wbwu/Filter.html).  His changes include:

* The proper handling of pole/zero. Now when one moves a pole on top of
  a zero, they will cancel each other. The numbers of poles and zeros are
  always equal, so if a pole is deleted, and there is more zero than pole,
  then a pole will be added at origin. When a user wants to add a term in
  the transfer function, he or she adds both pole/zero corresponding to
  that term.

* Support for complex conjugate poles and zeroes is added. So when user
  want to create a pair of Complex conjugate poles, he or she can do
  that at the add pole/zero dialog window. Once the pair is created,
  they are linked. (Pole and Zero uses the new PoleZero class, which is
  derived from Complex) When one pole, moves by the user, the other
  pole in the pair moves also. When one is deleted, the other will also
  be deleted. Also Math library also creates the conjugate link when
  creating real filter like Butterworth.

* The impulse response now shows both real and imaginary parts.


Web-Based Simulation of Embedded Software (UT Austin)
-----------------------------------------------------

Under subcontract, Brian Evans and his team at UT Austin have released
a new version of their extensible, configurable, portable, freely
distributable framework for Web-enabled simulation of embedded software
for DSPs and microcontrollers.  The new version, Version 1.0.6 (July 3,
1998), can be run by using a Java-enabled Web browser to open the URL

    http://anchovy.ece.utexas.edu/~arifler/wetics/

The source code can be downloaded from

    gftp://pepperoni.ece.utexas.edu/pub/wetics/

Version 1.0.6 of the Web-enabled Simulation framework consists of:

-- A set of Java applets that provide a configurable framework for
   Web-based user interfaces for instruction set architecture simulators.

-- A multithreaded TCP/IP Internet server written as a Java application
   that provides the interface between the Java applets and the simulators.

-- Command-line simulators/debuggers written in C/C++ for
   a. Texas Instruments TMS320C30 floating-point digital signal
      processor simulator
   b. Motorola MC68HC11 microcontroller simulator
   c. Motorola MC56811 fixed-point digital signal processing simulator
   d. Motorola MC56LC811 fixed-point digital signal processing board
      debugger

They provide pre-built binaries of the simulators and debuggers for
for Windows '95/NT and Solaris 2.5 machines.

New command-line tools for DSP and microcontroller processors and
boards can be added to the framework by only providing data about
the tools-- no Java applet or application code changes.   All of
the data is kept in one file.

They are in the process of adding security and privacy to their framework.

Their framework is in the spirit of the Web-based Electronic Design
(WELD) Project at UC Berkeley directed by Richard Newton. WELD "aims to
construct the first operational prototype of a national-scale CAD
design environment enabling Internet-wide IC design for the U.S.
electronics industry". WELD focuses on VLSI CAD tools and design
management infrastructure, and UT Austin's framework provides a
complementary focus on embedded software.


Automated Multi-Criteria Filter Optimization Framework (UT Austin)
------------------------------------------------------------------

Under subcontract, Brian Evans and his team at UT Austin have released
a set of Filter Optimization Packages for Matlab:

   http://www.ece.utexas.edu/~bevans/projects/syn_filter_software.html

The packages to optimize the following characteristics of analog filter
designs simultaneously

1. magnitude response 
2. linear phase in the passband
3. peak overshoot in the step response
4. quality factors (Q)

subject to constraints on the same characteristics. This framework
takes both behavioral and implementation characteristics of filters
into account. A traditional approach has been to design a filter
according to behavioral properties and then iteratively tweak the
filter poles and zeros for a given implementation technology. This
framework designs filters that simultaneously meet both behavioral and
implementation constraints and goals.

In terms of execution time on a 167-MHz Sun Ultra-2 workstation, the
Matlab packages take about 10 seconds to optimize fourth-order filters
and 3 minutes to optimize eighth-order filters.

Example: Design an analog lowpass filter with dp = 0.21 at wp = 20
  rad/sec and ds = 0.31 and ws = 30 rad/sec that has minimized deviation
  from linear phase in the passband and minimized peak overshoot in the
  step response, where the maximum quality factor of the second-order
  sections is 10.  We use the elliptic filter designed by Matlab to
  meet the magnitude constraints as the initial guess in the optimization.

          Elliptic Filter Designed by Matlab (Initial Guess)

        Q             Poles                   Zeros
      -----   ----------------------   ---------------------
       1.7    - 5.3553 +/- j 16.9547    0.0    +/- j 20.2479
      61      - 0.1636 +/- j 19.9899    0.0    +/- j 28.0184

      Refined Filter Returned by Filter Optimization Packages

        Q             Poles                   Zeros
      -----   ----------------------   ---------------------
       0.68   -11.4343 +/- j 10.5092   -3.4232 +/- j 28.6856
      10      - 1.0926 +/- j 21.8241   -1.2725 +/- j 35.5476

  The peak overshoot is reduced by 50% in the optimized filter,
  and the phase of the passband has become nearly linear.
  The initial elliptic filter cannot be built using breadboard
  components since the second second-order section has a quality
  factor of 61 (Q <= 25 is a practical upper limit for breadboard
  components).  The quality factor is the radius of the pole
  divided by -2 times the real part of the pole, so Q >= 0.5.
  A Q of 0.5 means a real pole, and an infinite Q means a pure 
  oscillator.  Lower Q is more desirable for filter implementation.
  When the optimization procedure is given an infeasible solution,
  the optimization routine searches for a feasible one, as it
  did in this case. [1]

The UT Austin team uses the symbolic mathematics environment
Mathematica to describe the constrained non-linear optimization problem
formally, derive the gradients of the cost function and constraints,
and synthesize the Matlab code to perform the optimization. In the
public release, they provide the Matlab to optimize analog IIR filters of
fourth, sixth, and eighth orders. Using the Mathematica formulation,
designers can add new measures and constraints, such as capacitance
spread for integrated circuit layout, and regenerate the Matlab code.

They describe the framework in [1]. An earlier version of the framework
is described in [2]. They plan to extend this framework to digital IIR
filters.


